export default {
  "p3-education-01": "I think there's some truth in that, but it's not that simple. On one hand, practical skills like financial literacy, coding and communication prepare students directly for the job market, which is clearly valuable. On the other hand, the key is that theoretical knowledge builds the foundation — you can't really apply science or economics without understanding the underlying concepts. So I'd say the system shouldn't abandon theory, but it should strike a better balance. For example, schools could combine textbook learning with internships or project-based tasks, so students see how abstract ideas work in real life.",
  "p3-education-02": "I'd say the main purpose is to develop independent thinking rather than simply to hand out qualifications. A university should train students to question assumptions, evaluate evidence and solve unfamiliar problems, because these skills stay with you long after specific facts are forgotten. It also acts as a stepping stone socially — many people build their professional networks there. That said, it largely depends on the individual: for some, it's about career preparation, while for others it's genuinely about intellectual growth. Ideally it serves both, but I think the thinking skills are the core.",
  "p3-education-03": "I don't think online learning will fully replace the classroom, although it will certainly reshape it. On the whole, online platforms are brilliant for flexibility and access — you can learn from top universities without leaving home. However, the key is that education is also a social process: students learn from debates, body language and spontaneous questions, which are hard to replicate on a screen. So I'd say the future is blended. Traditional schools might shift their role towards discussion and mentoring, while routine content delivery moves online, but the physical classroom won't disappear entirely.",
  "p3-education-04": "It depends on what you're measuring. Exams are efficient at testing knowledge recall and performance under pressure, and they provide a standardised benchmark that's easy to compare. However, I think they're quite limited when it comes to creativity, teamwork or long-term understanding — a student can memorise material and forget it a week later. So I'd argue the most effective approach is a mixture: continuous assessment, projects and portfolios alongside exams. That way, you capture both what students know and how they actually apply it, which reflects their real progress much better.",
  "p3-education-05": "I think this argument comes from the idea that theory gives you transferable understanding. If you understand the underlying principles of, say, physics or economics, you can adapt to new situations, whereas a purely practical skill can become obsolete when technology changes. There's also a historical element — universities were traditionally about cultivating the mind, not training workers. That said, I'd say this view is gradually weakening, because employers increasingly value what graduates can actually do. The key is that theory and practice are complementary; the debate only exists because people frame them as opposites.",
  "p3-education-06": "I'd say yes, at least for compulsory schooling, because education is a public good that benefits society as a whole — a more educated population tends to mean higher productivity and lower crime. However, free university education is more complicated, since it largely depends on a country's budget and priorities. Some countries fund it through general taxation, while others use loans or means-testing, and both can work. I think the key is that nobody should be excluded from education because of their family's income, even if the funding model needs to be carefully designed to remain sustainable.",
  "p3-society-01": "An ageing population creates several significant pressures. On the practical side, the healthcare and pension systems come under enormous strain, because fewer working-age people are paying taxes while more people need costly medical care. There's also a labour shortage, which can slow economic growth, and a care burden that often falls on families. I'd say the social dimension matters too — if older people are isolated and younger people are overworked, the whole society feels the tension. The key is that governments need to plan ahead, for example by raising the retirement age gradually or encouraging immigration.",
  "p3-society-02": "I think the main driver is opportunity. Cities concentrate jobs, universities and services, so for young people especially, moving to a city often feels like the only way to build a career. There's also the lifestyle factor — cities offer entertainment, restaurants, cultural events and a more diverse social scene, which many people find attractive. The downside, of course, is the cost, but on the whole people accept that trade-off. I'd say it largely depends on the stage of life: people in their twenties tend to chase opportunity, while some families eventually move out for space and quiet.",
  "p3-society-03": "There's no single solution, but I'd say the most effective levers are taxation and public services. A progressive tax system, where higher earners contribute proportionally more, can fund education, healthcare and housing support that give poorer families a genuine chance to move up. The key is that inequality isn't just about income — it's about opportunity, so early childhood programmes and affordable education matter a lot. Of course, policies like this are politically difficult, and it largely depends on a country's culture and economy, but I think targeted investment in the poorest communities is the most promising route.",
  "p3-society-04": "I'd say yes, and it's a mixed story. On one hand, traditional neighbourhood connections have weakened — people often don't know their neighbours the way previous generations did, partly because we're busier and partly because screens fill our social time. On the other hand, technology has created new communities based on shared interests rather than geography, so people aren't necessarily less connected overall. The key is that these connections tend to be shallower and more transactional. So on the whole, I think local community bonds have declined, even though we're technically in touch with more people than ever.",
  "p3-society-05": "I think the benefits are substantial. Culturally, a multicultural society exposes you to different food, music, art and traditions, which makes everyday life richer and broadens your perspective. There's also an economic argument — diverse teams tend to be more innovative because they approach problems from different angles, and international connections can boost trade. On a deeper level, I'd say living alongside people who are different teaches tolerance and reduces prejudice, which is valuable in a globalised world. Of course, integration isn't always smooth, but on the whole the diversity of ideas is a genuine strength.",
  "p3-society-06": "I'd say the starting point is accessibility, both physical and digital — if public transport, buildings and websites are designed inclusively from the beginning, many barriers disappear without extra cost. Beyond that, the key is meaningful employment and social inclusion rather than charity: giving people the chance to work and participate builds dignity and independence. Education and awareness also matter, because a lot of exclusion comes from ignorance rather than bad intent. It largely depends on government funding, but I think the principle is clear — society should remove the barriers, not expect vulnerable groups to overcome them alone.",
  "p3-environment-01": "I think it's a shared responsibility, but governments have to lead because individual action alone can't solve systemic problems. A person can recycle and cycle to work, yet if the country runs on coal and builds everything around cars, those efforts barely register. So governments should create the framework — regulations, carbon pricing, clean energy investment — that makes green choices the easy choices. That said, individuals matter too, because public pressure shapes policy. I'd say the key is that governments set the rules, but citizens have to demand them and change their own habits alongside.",
  "p3-environment-02": "There are quite a few practical steps, and the biggest ones are usually in transport and energy. Using public transport, cycling or walking instead of driving makes a real difference, as does reducing flying where possible. In the home, people can cut energy use by insulating, using efficient appliances and simply turning things off. Diet also plays a role — eating less meat and wasting less food are surprisingly impactful. I'd say the key is to focus on the big sources rather than tiny gestures, and to make changes that are sustainable long-term, because a small habit you keep beats a dramatic gesture you abandon.",
  "p3-environment-03": "I think it's mostly a question of convenience and habit rather than ignorance. If recycling bins are far away, or the rules are confusing — which plastic goes where, whether the lid should be on — people tend to give up and just throw everything in one bin. There's also a psychological factor: one person's recycling feels like a drop in the ocean, so people doubt it matters. I'd say the key is that systems should make recycling the default, not the extra effort. When it's genuinely easy, most people do it without thinking; when it's a hassle, even the well-intentioned skip it.",
  "p3-environment-04": "I think the effects will become increasingly tangible. On a basic level, more frequent extreme weather — heatwaves, floods and storms — will disrupt daily routines, from commuting to food prices, since agriculture is highly sensitive to climate. Water and energy costs may rise, and some coastal communities will face genuine relocation pressure. Health could be affected too, with hotter summers and new disease patterns. I'd say the key is that these changes won't be dramatic all at once; they'll creep into everyday life gradually, which is exactly why people underestimate them. Adapting our cities and habits now is much cheaper than reacting later.",
  "p3-environment-05": "Yes, I'd say they should, because companies have far more power to prevent pollution than individuals do, and legal responsibility creates a real incentive to change. If a factory knows it will be fined for dumping waste, it will invest in cleaner technology; if not, cutting corners is simply cheaper. The key is that the responsibility should include the whole supply chain, not just what happens at the factory gate, and the penalties need to be strong enough to hurt. That said, regulation has to be fair and predictable, so businesses can plan, rather than being hit by sudden or arbitrary rules.",
  "p3-environment-06": "Because the environment doesn't respect borders. Carbon emissions from one country warm the whole planet, and deforestation or ocean pollution in one region affects ecosystems everywhere, so no single nation can solve these problems alone. There's also an economic logic: if one country imposes strict green rules while its competitors don't, it loses business and the pollution just moves elsewhere — a problem called carbon leakage. I'd say the key is shared agreements with clear targets, like the Paris Agreement, combined with financial support so developing countries can afford to go green. Without cooperation, everyone free-rides and everyone loses.",
  "p3-technology-01": "AI has quietly woven itself into both spheres. At work, it automates repetitive tasks like data entry, drafting and translation, which frees people for more creative work — though it also makes some roles redundant. In daily life, recommendation systems shape what we watch and buy, and chatbots now handle customer service around the clock. I'd say the key is that AI's biggest effect is on decision-making: it increasingly decides what we see and even helps doctors or judges analyse cases. It's a double-edged sword — enormous efficiency gains, but real questions about privacy and bias that society is still catching up with.",
  "p3-technology-02": "I think the effects are mixed but worrying in some areas. Too much passive screen time — endless videos or games — can cut into sleep, physical activity and face-to-face interaction, which are all crucial for development. Attention spans may suffer, because content is designed to be instantly rewarding. On the other hand, screens aren't all bad: educational apps and video calls with relatives can be genuinely beneficial. I'd say the key is quality and balance rather than total bans. A child who uses a tablet for an hour of creative or educational content is very different from one who spends the whole day passively scrolling.",
  "p3-technology-03": "I'd say it's a genuine debate, but I lean towards optimism with conditions. Historically, every wave of automation destroyed some jobs but created entirely new industries — before the internet, nobody had 'social media manager' as a career. So in the long run, I think technology tends to create more, but the problem is the transition: displaced workers need retraining, and that takes time and investment. The key is that the jobs of the future will require different skills — creativity, emotional intelligence, complex problem-solving — and societies that invest in education will benefit far more than those that simply hope for the best.",
  "p3-technology-04": "It depends very much on the age and the purpose. For a young child, a basic phone for contacting parents seems sensible, but a full smartphone with unrestricted internet is a different matter entirely. The risks are real — exposure to inappropriate content, social pressure and disrupted sleep — and children's self-regulation simply isn't developed yet. I'd say the key is not the device itself but the boundaries around it: clear time limits, open conversations about what they see online, and parents modelling healthy screen use. When it's managed well, a smartphone can be a useful tool; when it's not, it becomes a source of anxiety.",
  "p3-technology-05": "I think the first step is awareness — most people hand over data without reading what they agree to. Practically, people can use strong, unique passwords with a password manager, enable two-factor authentication, and check privacy settings on social media instead of leaving them on default. It's also wise to be selective about which apps get access to contacts and location. That said, I'd argue individuals can only do so much, because companies collect data by design. So the key is a combination: personal caution plus pressure on governments to enforce strong data protection laws.",
  "p3-technology-06": "I agree to a large extent. We've become used to instant gratification — streaming loads instantly, messages arrive immediately — so anything that takes more than a few seconds feels frustrating. And distraction is almost built into our devices, with notifications constantly interrupting our focus. I notice it myself: it's harder to read a long article or watch a film without reaching for the phone. That said, I'd say it's a habit rather than a permanent change. People can retrain their attention through deliberate practice — leaving the phone in another room, turning off notifications — and the fact that we can makes me less pessimistic.",
  "p3-work-01": "I think the first thing is setting boundaries, because work tends to expand to fill whatever space you give it. That means clear working hours, not answering emails late at night, and actually taking holidays rather than feeling guilty about them. It also helps to be realistic about what 'balance' means — it doesn't have to be equal time, just the right mix for your stage of life. Employers play a role too: flexible hours and remote options make balance far more achievable. I'd say the key is that balance is something you actively design, not something that happens by accident.",
  "p3-work-02": "I'd say no — not most jobs, but large parts of many jobs. Automation is excellent at repetitive, predictable tasks, whether it's factory assembly or processing standard documents, and those roles are genuinely at risk. However, jobs that require empathy, creativity, judgement and physical adaptability — nursing, teaching, plumbing — are much harder to automate. The key is that automation usually transforms jobs rather than simply erasing them: a radiologist still reads scans, but AI helps flag suspicious ones. So I think the future is humans working alongside machines, with the real challenge being retraining people whose tasks disappear.",
  "p3-work-03": "I think the advantages are real but unevenly shared. Working from home saves commuting time and money, offers flexibility, and for many people actually improves focus and productivity. On the other hand, the disadvantages include isolation — without informal office contact, collaboration can suffer, and younger employees especially miss out on learning by watching others. There's also the problem of boundaries: the home becomes the office, and switching off becomes harder, which can lead to burnout. I'd say the ideal is a hybrid model, which combines the flexibility of remote work with the social and mentoring benefits of the office.",
  "p3-work-04": "I think there are several reasons, and attitudes have genuinely shifted. People live and work longer, so the old idea of one career for forty years feels unrealistic — and frankly, monotonous. Many reach a point where they question whether the salary justifies the stress, and they look for something more meaningful or better balanced. Technology also makes it easier: online courses and remote work mean you can retrain without giving up your income overnight. I'd say the key is that society now accepts career change as normal, so people feel freer to take the risk, whereas a generation ago it would have seemed irresponsible.",
  "p3-work-05": "It depends on the individual, but I'd say for most people, satisfaction matters more in the long run. A high salary can't really compensate for dreading Monday mornings, and research consistently shows that once basic needs are covered, extra income adds surprisingly little happiness. On the other hand, I think it's easy to romanticise this when you have financial security — someone struggling to pay rent may quite reasonably choose the better-paid job first. The key is that it's a spectrum, not a choice: the ideal is a job that pays enough to live comfortably and offers some meaning. The balance shifts at different life stages.",
  "p3-work-06": "I'd say they should be encouraged to consider it, but not pushed into it as if it's the only respectable path. Entrepreneurship teaches invaluable skills — resilience, self-discipline, problem-solving — even if the business fails, and the best age to take that risk is arguably when you have few obligations. However, most businesses genuinely do fail, and stable employment provides security and experience that can make later entrepreneurship more successful. I think the key is exposure: young people should learn about both paths, try small ventures while studying, and then decide based on their own temperament, because not everyone is cut out to be a founder, and that's perfectly fine.",
  "p3-media-01": "Social media shapes opinions in ways that are both obvious and subtle. Obviously, the algorithm decides what you see, so your feed tends to confirm what you already believe, which hardens views rather than challenging them. More subtly, social proof plays a big role — when a post has thousands of likes, people tend to accept it more readily, regardless of its accuracy. I'd say the key is that social media rewards emotion over nuance: outrageous content spreads fastest, so moderate, careful opinions get drowned out. The result is that people form strong opinions quickly, but often with less evidence than they'd need in face-to-face debate.",
  "p3-media-02": "I think trust comes down to a combination of track record, transparency and familiarity. People trust outlets that have a history of accurate reporting, and they trust sources they can verify — for example, a newspaper that corrects its mistakes earns more respect than one that never admits them. There's also a tribal element: people tend to trust sources that share their political leanings, even when they'd never admit that's the reason. I'd say the key is that trust is built slowly and destroyed quickly. One false story in an outlet people already doubt is enough to make them switch, which is why reputation is a news organisation's most valuable asset.",
  "p3-media-03": "I think it should be heavily restricted, and in some cases banned. Children don't have the cognitive ability to understand that adverts are trying to persuade them — a six-year-old can't really distinguish between a cartoon and a commercial for sugary cereal. That makes advertising to them inherently manipulative, which is why many countries already restrict it during children's programming. On the other hand, I'd say a total ban is hard to enforce in the digital age, since kids see influencer content that functions as advertising anyway. So the key is stricter rules on junk food, and better media literacy teaching children to recognise persuasion early.",
  "p3-media-04": "I'd say the printed newspaper will largely disappear, but traditional media as a whole will survive in a new form. The economics are clear: printing and distribution are expensive, and almost everyone now reads news on screens, so the paper version is becoming a niche product. However, the journalistic values of traditional media — fact-checking, professional reporting, editorial judgement — are more valuable than ever in the age of misinformation. I think the key is that the medium changes but the function survives: established outlets are increasingly becoming trusted digital brands, and the ones that adapt will thrive, while those that cling to print will indeed disappear.",
  "p3-media-05": "The dangers are serious and quite far-reaching. At an individual level, fake news can mislead people into harmful decisions — taking unproven medical treatments, for example, or falling for scams that look like official announcements. At a societal level, it erodes trust: when people can't agree on basic facts, public debate breaks down, and that's exactly how conspiracy theories and political polarisation flourish. I'd also say there's a dangerous 'lying in broad daylight' effect — as false stories spread faster than corrections, the lie gets a head start that the truth never quite catches up with. That's why media literacy matters so much.",
  "p3-media-06": "I think the change is fundamental. A decade ago, most people got news from fixed times — the morning paper, the evening bulletin — but now news arrives constantly through phones and social media, and it's personalised by algorithms. This means people increasingly see a version of the world tailored to their interests and biases. There's also been a shift in format: short videos and headlines compete for attention, so people often skim rather than read deeply. I'd say the key consequence is that consuming news is now a passive, constant background activity, whereas it used to be a deliberate act, and that has real implications for how informed people actually are.",
  "p3-health-01": "I'd say their effectiveness is real but limited, and it depends on what they're trying to change. Campaigns are quite good at raising awareness — everyone now knows smoking is dangerous — but awareness doesn't always translate into action, especially when the behaviour is enjoyable or addictive. The most effective campaigns combine information with structural change: anti-smoking worked far better once taxes rose and indoor smoking was banned. I think the key is that a poster or an advert can shift attitudes slowly, but lasting behaviour change usually needs the environment to make healthy choices easier, which is where policy comes in.",
  "p3-health-02": "I think there are several overlapping reasons. First, the stigma has genuinely reduced — people are more willing to talk openly about anxiety and depression, partly because public figures and celebrities have shared their own experiences. Second, there's better scientific understanding, so mental health is now treated as a medical issue rather than a character flaw. Third, modern life may genuinely be more stressful — constant connectivity, job insecurity and social media comparison — so the problem is more visible. I'd say the awareness itself is a positive development, but it's important that it leads to real support services, not just conversations, because awareness without access to help has its limits.",
  "p3-health-03": "I think the biggest levers are funding and geography. If healthcare is paid for at the point of use, poorer families delay treatment until problems become serious, so governments can make services free or subsidised — many countries do this through general taxation. Geography matters too: rural areas often lack specialists, so telemedicine and mobile clinics can bring care to people who would otherwise travel for hours. I'd say the key is prevention rather than cure — investing in vaccination, screening and public health education is far cheaper than treating advanced disease. No system is perfect, but accessibility is ultimately a political choice about priorities.",
  "p3-health-04": "On the whole, yes, but in a complicated way. We're healthier in terms of life expectancy and infectious disease — we no longer die of cholera or measles, and modern medicine treats conditions that used to be fatal. However, lifestyle diseases have replaced infectious ones: obesity, diabetes and heart problems linked to processed food and sedentary work are now epidemic. There's also the mental health dimension, which is arguably worse than before. So I'd say people today are healthier in medical terms but face a different set of problems, and whether you call that 'healthier' really depends on whether you're counting physical survival or overall wellbeing.",
  "p3-health-05": "I'd say yes, but with caution. The evidence from sugar taxes, for example in Mexico, suggests they do reduce consumption, and the revenue can fund public health programmes — so it's a sensible nudge rather than a ban. However, there's a fairness problem: such taxes tend to hit poorer households hardest, because cheap, unhealthy food is what they can afford. So I think the key is that a tax should be part of a package — subsidising fruit and vegetables, regulating advertising to children, and improving food labelling — otherwise it just punishes people with few choices instead of actually making healthy options easier.",
  "p3-health-06": "I think schools are actually the ideal place for this, because they reach children at the age when habits form. Practically, they can increase physical activity — more sports time, active breaks, and safe places to play — and improve school meals, since children eat a large share of their food there. Education matters too, but not just as facts in a textbook: cooking classes and gardening projects make healthy choices tangible and fun. I'd say the key is consistency between what schools teach and what they model — if a school preaches healthy eating but sells only sugary snacks in the canteen, children quickly learn the lesson is empty.",
  "p3-city-life-01": "I think the key is making public transport the easy option, not the virtuous one. That means frequency and reliability first — people tolerate waiting if the bus comes every five minutes, but a thirty-minute gap pushes everyone back into cars. Pricing matters too: integrated ticketing, where one card covers bus, metro and bike share, removes the friction. I'd also say safety and comfort shouldn't be underestimated, especially for women and older people travelling at night. And it has to be genuinely faster than driving: dedicated bus lanes and priority signalling make the comparison obvious, and then the habit change follows naturally.",
  "p3-city-life-02": "I'd say it's fundamentally a mismatch between supply and demand. Cities attract jobs and people, so demand keeps rising, but housing supply hasn't kept pace — planning restrictions, slow approvals and land costs all make new construction difficult and expensive. There's also an investment dimension: in many cities, property is treated as a financial asset rather than a place to live, so investors buy apartments they never occupy, pushing prices up further. I think the key is that this isn't a natural law — it's a policy choice. Cities that build aggressively, like Vienna with its social housing, show that affordability can be protected with political will.",
  "p3-city-life-03": "I'd say it largely depends on what you value, so there's no universal answer. Cities win on opportunity, variety and convenience — jobs, education, culture, and services all within reach — which is why young people flood in. The countryside offers space, nature, quiet and community, which many people find they crave later in life. I think the honest answer is that both have real costs: the city taxes your wallet and your peace of mind, while the countryside can limit your options and your income. So it's not that one is better — it's that they suit different people and different stages of life.",
  "p3-city-life-04": "I think they're important on several levels that people often underestimate. Physically, they're the city's lungs — trees cool the streets, absorb pollution and manage rainwater — and they're the only place many residents can exercise outdoors. Psychologically, green space measurably reduces stress: studies consistently show that people who can see trees or visit parks report better mental health. Socially, parks are one of the few free, neutral spaces where different groups mix — families, elderly people, joggers all share the same ground. I'd say the key is that parks are not luxury decoration; they're essential infrastructure, which is why the best cities protect them fiercely.",
  "p3-city-life-05": "I think the most effective measures make driving less attractive rather than just building more roads, which tends to fill up again. Congestion charging, like London's, prices cars out of the centre and funds public transport. Parking policy matters too — if parking is expensive and scarce, people think twice about driving. Meanwhile, cities need to offer a real alternative: reliable metro and bus networks, cycle lanes that feel safe, and walking-friendly streets. I'd say the key is that no single measure works alone; it's the combination that changes behaviour. And the goal shouldn't be zero cars, just fewer cars used efficiently.",
  "p3-city-life-06": "The most obvious drawback is cost — housing, and often everything else, is significantly more expensive, which puts enormous pressure on people's finances. There's also the daily friction of crowds and commuting; large cities mean long journeys and constant noise, which quietly wears people down. I'd say the social dimension is often overlooked: it's easy to feel anonymous and isolated in a city of millions, especially for newcomers. And on top of that, pollution levels in megacities can genuinely affect health. So while cities offer enormous opportunities, they demand a lot in return — money, time and resilience — and that trade-off isn't right for everyone.",
  "p3-culture-01": "Yes, I'd say it's important, but preservation shouldn't mean freezing culture in the past. Traditional culture carries identity — language, festivals, crafts and stories connect people to their history and to each other, and that sense of belonging matters in a world that changes fast. There's also a diversity argument: every tradition lost is a way of understanding the world gone forever. However, the key is that cultures have always evolved, and the most resilient traditions adapt while keeping their core. So I think the goal is to pass on the essence — the values and knowledge — while allowing the forms to change naturally.",
  "p3-culture-02": "I think governments have the resources, but individuals have the daily influence, and both are needed. Governments can fund conservation, protect historic sites with law, support museums and subsidise traditional crafts and languages that the market alone wouldn't sustain. Individuals matter in more subtle ways: actually visiting heritage sites, buying from local craftspeople, teaching children the language and customs at home. The key is that heritage only survives if it's used — a festival kept alive by one community is far more durable than a monument locked behind gates. So I'd say protection is really about creating demand and participation, not just spending money on restoration.",
  "p3-culture-03": "I'd say it's a real risk, but 'destroying' is too absolute. Globalisation does create powerful pressure towards uniformity — the same brands, films and fast food appear everywhere, and local traditions can struggle to compete for attention. However, I think the picture is more complex: globalisation also gives local cultures tools to reach new audiences, so a regional cuisine or dialect can gain followers worldwide. The key is that what often gets destroyed is not culture itself but its casual, everyday practice. So the danger is real, but it's not inevitable — it depends on whether communities consciously value and maintain their distinct traditions.",
  "p3-culture-04": "I think it's mostly a competition for attention. Young people's cultural life is now dominated by global, digital content — streaming, social media, international trends — which is designed to be endlessly engaging, while traditional festivals can feel old-fashioned and less exciting by comparison. There's also a practical factor: in many families, grandparents were the ones who taught customs, but with busier lives and greater geographical distance, that transmission is thinning. I'd say the key is that young people aren't rejecting their culture so much as not being exposed to it in meaningful ways. When festivals are made interactive and fun, interest often returns quickly.",
  "p3-culture-05": "I think the benefits operate at several levels. On a personal level, cultural exchange breaks down stereotypes — when you actually meet people from a different culture, the abstract images you had get replaced by real, individual faces. On a creative level, exchange drives innovation: cuisine, music, design and even science advance when ideas cross borders and combine. There's also a diplomatic benefit, because people-to-people connections make international conflict harder to sustain. I'd say the key is that cultural exchange builds empathy in a way that reading about other cultures never quite achieves — which is precisely why programmes like student exchanges are so valuable.",
  "p3-culture-06": "I'd say ideally yes, because museums are educational institutions, not just attractions — charging for entry means children from poorer families may never step inside, and that's a real social cost. Several major cities have already made their museums free and seen visitor numbers soar, including among local families who previously never went. However, the reality is that maintenance is expensive, and free entry needs government funding to work. So I think the key is a balance: free access to the core collections, with charges for special exhibitions and events, so that the institution stays sustainable while the door stays open to everyone.",
  "p3-family-01": "I think the change has been profound, though uneven. The most visible shift is that the old model — the man as sole breadwinner, the woman as full-time homemaker — has largely broken down: in most countries, both partners work, and childcare and housework are far more shared than before. There's also been a cultural change in expectations: fathers are now expected to be emotionally involved, not just financial providers. That said, I'd argue the reality still lags behind the ideal — studies consistently show women still do more unpaid domestic work. So the roles have genuinely changed, but the pace has been faster in theory than in practice.",
  "p3-family-02": "Yes, I'd say so, but independence has to be built gradually and matched to the child's age. Small responsibilities — dressing themselves, packing their school bag, managing pocket money — teach confidence and problem-solving, and children who never face manageable challenges tend to struggle later when the real ones arrive. However, the key is the word 'encourage': independence shouldn't mean abandonment. The best approach, I think, is giving children a safe space to try, fail and try again, while parents remain a supportive safety net. That balance — pushing gently without hovering — is harder than it sounds, but it's what builds genuinely self-reliant adults.",
  "p3-family-03": "I'd say it should be a partnership, not a single owner. Family members naturally carry the emotional responsibility, and in many cultures taking care of parents is simply expected — but expecting families to do everything alone is unrealistic, especially when both adult children work. Governments and communities have to provide the support structures: affordable care services, home visits, and financial help for family carers. The key is that the burden shouldn't rest on one person, and it shouldn't be all-or-nothing — many families would love to care for their elders but need help with the heavy lifting. So I think responsibility is genuinely shared, and pretending otherwise harms both the elderly and their families.",
  "p3-family-04": "On the whole, I'd say no, and it's mostly a question of time scarcity rather than lack of love. In most families both parents work long hours, children have school plus activities, and even when everyone is at home, screens compete for attention — it's common for a family to be in the same room but each absorbed in their own device. The key is that quality time doesn't just happen; it has to be designed. Things like shared meals without phones, or a weekly outing, make a real difference. So I think families are close in intention but often thin on genuine, undistracted time together.",
  "p3-family-05": "There are quite a few, starting with practical support. Grandparents can help with childcare while parents work, and household costs can be shared, which matters in expensive cities. There's also an emotional layer: children grow up with grandparents and cousins, learning from a wider range of ages and perspectives, and older relatives feel less isolated — it's a natural safety net. On the other hand, I should acknowledge the downsides — less privacy and potential conflicts — but the advantages are real. I'd say the key is that extended family living works best when there are clear boundaries and mutual respect, which is why it functions beautifully in some households and not others.",
  "p3-family-06": "I think the honest answer is that it requires both personal strategy and workplace flexibility, because no individual habit can fix an inflexible job. Personally, parents can protect rituals — family dinner, weekend time — and accept that perfection is impossible; the goal is good enough, not seamless. Professionally, the key is flexibility: remote work, flexible hours or job-sharing can make an enormous difference, which is why companies that offer these retain happier employees. I'd also say it's important to share the load fairly between partners, because when one person carries everything, burnout is inevitable. Balance here is less about dividing time equally and more about being present when it matters.",
  "p3-consumption-01": "I think it's a combination of psychology and design. Modern shopping is engineered to bypass rational thought: one-click buying, endless discounts, and targeted ads that appear moments after you mention something out loud. There's also a social element — social media constantly displays other people's purchases, which triggers comparison and the sense that you're missing out. On a deeper level, I'd say shopping has become a way to regulate mood — a quick purchase gives a small dopamine hit when people feel stressed or bored. The key is that the ease of buying has raced ahead of our self-control, and the whole system is designed to exploit that gap.",
  "p3-consumption-02": "The effects have been dramatic, especially for small independent retailers. Online shopping offers convenience, price comparison and delivery to the door, so high streets have lost foot traffic, and we've seen waves of closures of bookshops, clothes stores and electronics shops. However, I'd say the story isn't entirely negative — traditional shops that adapt can thrive by offering what the internet can't: experience, immediacy and personal service. Cafés, boutiques and shops with strong local identity are doing well precisely because they're destinations, not just suppliers. The key is that online and offline are settling into complementary roles rather than the internet simply winning everything.",
  "p3-consumption-03": "I'd say it's not that young people are more irresponsible — it's that the conditions are different. Older generations grew up with cash, which is more tangible; you literally watch the notes disappear from your wallet, whereas card payments and contactless spending are almost invisible. Young people also face an economy designed to extract money from them — subscription services, in-app purchases, influencer marketing — that simply didn't exist before. On top of that, wages have stagnated relative to housing costs, so there's often less left to save. So I think the comparison is unfair: it's largely about the environment, not the character, of the generations.",
  "p3-consumption-04": "I think the first step is visibility — people overspend largely because they don't really know where their money goes. Budgeting apps or simply tracking expenses for a month can be surprisingly revealing. There are also practical tricks: a waiting period before buying non-essentials, unsubscribing from marketing emails, and paying with cash or a set amount rather than a card, since spending feels more real that way. I'd say the deeper key is distinguishing wants from needs and questioning the emotional trigger — whether it's stress, boredom or social pressure — before reaching for the wallet. It's not about deprivation; it's about making spending deliberate rather than automatic.",
  "p3-consumption-05": "Absolutely — that's essentially its job. Advertising doesn't mainly inform people about products they already need; it creates perceived needs by linking products to emotions and identity. A watch isn't sold as a timepiece but as a symbol of success; a drink is sold as happiness or belonging. There's also the sheer volume of it — people now see thousands of adverts a day, including disguised ones from influencers, so the pressure is constant. I'd say the key is that advertising is most powerful when people don't realise they're being advertised to. The moment you notice the mechanism, its grip weakens considerably, which is why media literacy is such a useful defence.",
  "p3-consumption-06": "Yes, I'd strongly say so, and I think it's one of the most important changes consumers should push for. 'Planned obsolescence' — designing products to fail or feel outdated — is wasteful and environmentally destructive, creating endless mountains of e-waste. If phones were designed to last ten years and be repairable, consumers would save money and the environmental cost would drop dramatically. The obstacle is economic: companies profit from frequent replacement, and cheap products seduce consumers who don't consider lifetime costs. So I think the key is regulation plus a cultural shift — repairability standards, longer warranties, and consumers valuing durability over novelty — because the current system serves nobody but the manufacturers.",
  "p3-travel-01": "I think tourism is genuinely a double-edged sword. On the positive side, it brings income, jobs and infrastructure — hotels, restaurants and airports — that can transform a struggling local economy, and it can revive interest in local crafts and culture. On the negative side, mass tourism can push up housing prices, crowd out everyday life, and put enormous strain on water and waste systems, as we've seen in places like Venice or Barcelona. There's also the cultural damage when traditions are performed for tourists rather than lived. I'd say the key is scale and management — tourism done thoughtfully benefits everyone, but tourism allowed to run unchecked gradually destroys the very thing visitors came to see.",
  "p3-travel-02": "I think travel works on several levels. Most obviously, it breaks the assumption that your way of doing things is the only way — seeing how other people eat, work, worship and organise family life makes your own customs visible as choices rather than facts. There's also the practical dimension: navigating unfamiliar places, languages and problems builds confidence and adaptability. And I'd say the deepest effect is humility — when you're a guest in another culture, you learn to listen and observe rather than assume. Of course, that only happens if you travel with an open mind; a tourist who just checks off sights can come home essentially unchanged.",
  "p3-travel-03": "I think the starting point is shifting from quantity to quality. Instead of attracting millions of short-stay visitors who fly in for a weekend, destinations can target fewer tourists who stay longer and spend locally — which reduces pressure and brings in comparable revenue. Practical measures include limiting visitor numbers at fragile sites, investing in rail over short-haul flights, and supporting local accommodation and food rather than international chains. There's also a role for tourists themselves: choosing slower, closer travel and respecting local ecosystems. I'd say the key is that sustainability has to be built into the business model, not added as an afterthought, because voluntary good intentions alone won't survive commercial pressure.",
  "p3-travel-04": "I think it comes down to freedom and depth of experience. Solo travellers can change plans on a whim — stay an extra day somewhere beautiful, skip the crowded museum, eat dinner when they feel like it — whereas group travel involves constant compromise. There's also a personal-growth element: travelling alone forces you to rely on yourself, talk to strangers and sit with your own thoughts, which many people find genuinely rewarding. And contrary to what people expect, solo travel isn't necessarily lonely — hostels and tours make it easy to meet others, but you can also choose solitude. I'd say it's really a personality thing: some people find solitude energising, while others need company to enjoy anything.",
  "p3-travel-05": "Technology has transformed both ends of the journey. Planning used to mean travel agents and paper guidebooks; now everything — flights, hotels, routes, local recommendations — is aggregated into apps, and reviews mean you rarely book blind. During the trip, the change is even more profound: GPS navigation removed the fear of getting lost, translation apps bridge language gaps instantly, and smartphones hold tickets, maps and cameras in one device. However, I'd say there's a downside: the phone can become a barrier, with people photographing experiences rather than living them, and following algorithm suggestions instead of wandering. The key is using technology as a tool without letting it replace spontaneity.",
  "p3-travel-06": "Yes, I think limits are necessary in certain places, though they should be smart rather than blunt. Iconic sites like Machu Picchu or the Galápagos are physically fragile, and uncontrolled visitor numbers cause real damage to both the environment and the experience — crowds make a wonder of the world feel like a queue at an airport. Managed approaches work: timed entry slots, daily caps, higher fees in peak season, and promoting lesser-known alternatives nearby. I'd say the key is that limits don't have to mean rejecting tourism; they mean managing it so the destination survives long-term. Without them, 'overtourism' ends up destroying the very appeal the economy depends on.",
  "p3-tech-society-01": "I'd say it's complicated, because technology affects sociability in both directions. On one hand, there's a real cost: when everyone in a café is looking at a phone, conversation dies, and people increasingly choose to message rather than meet. On the other hand, technology also creates sociability — it's how many people maintain long-distance friendships and organise real gatherings, and for shy people it can be a gentler way to connect. I think the key is that technology amplifies whatever tendency you already have: an outgoing person uses it to socialise more, while someone prone to isolation can retreat further. So the device itself is neutral; it's how we use it that matters.",
  "p3-tech-society-02": "Definitely not, and I think most parents and experts would agree that unlimited is never sensible. Unrestricted screen time tends to crowd out sleep, physical activity and face-to-face interaction, all of which are essential for children's development, and it exposes them to content they aren't ready for. However, I'd caution against the opposite extreme of rigid bans, which often backfire and leave children unprepared for a digital world. The key, I think, is age-appropriate limits combined with quality: active, creative use — learning, making, creating — deserves more leeway than passive scrolling, and parents should gradually hand over self-management as children get older. Balance and guidance, not absolutism, is the answer.",
  "p3-tech-society-03": "Technology has changed the mechanics of friendship dramatically. Building friendships is now easier in some ways — shared interests can connect you with people across the world, and online communities form around anything imaginable. Maintaining friendships has also become lighter and more constant: a message, a like, a shared meme keeps the thread alive between real meetings, which wasn't possible when a friendship depended on phone calls or letters. However, I'd say there's a trade-off — these lightweight contacts can substitute for deeper interaction, giving a false sense of connection. The key is that technology maintains the surface of friendships brilliantly, but the depth still has to be built in person, through shared time and real conversations.",
  "p3-tech-society-04": "I think the online world offers connection without its costs. Online, you can curate your identity, control the pace of interaction, and always find people who share your niche interests — it's low-risk and immediately rewarding. Real-life relationships, by contrast, require effort, vulnerability and tolerance of awkwardness, which is why the gap can grow: the more time people invest in effortless online connection, the less practice and confidence they have for the harder, messier real thing. There's also a comparison effect — seeing everyone's happy online lives can make your own quiet reality feel worse. So I'd say online connection can become a substitute that slowly weakens the very social skills it claims to serve.",
  "p3-tech-society-05": "I'd say it's easier in one sense and harder in another, which is an uncomfortable combination. It's easier because anyone can publish instantly — you no longer need a platform or permission to say what you think, and anonymity can free people who fear judgement. But it's harder because the real stakes are higher: your opinion is permanent, visible to employers and relatives, and subject to immediate public reaction, including harassment. That pushes many people toward self-censorship — sharing only safe opinions — or toward aggressive performative ones for likes. I think the key is that social media rewards strong reactions over honest nuance, so the 'true' opinion people express is often the one that performs best, not the one they privately hold.",
  "p3-tech-society-06": "The most visible consequence is the erosion of non-verbal communication skills. When most interaction happens through text, people lose practice reading body language, tone and facial expression, which are essential for real relationships and teamwork. There's also a subtler effect on expectations: digital communication is instant and asynchronous, so people become impatient with the pace of real conversation and expect replies at all hours. And I'd argue there's a community-level cost — digital interaction tends to be transactional and interest-based, so the casual, unplanned encounters that build neighbourhoods and social trust decline. The key is that digital communication is a powerful supplement, but when it becomes the primary mode, something important about how humans connect quietly gets lost.",
  "p3-city-rural-01": "I think the main push is opportunity — or the lack of it. Rural areas often have fewer jobs, lower wages and limited education options, so young people especially feel they have to leave to build any kind of career. There's also a pull from the city: better healthcare, entertainment and social life, which matters a lot to people in their twenties. Beyond economics, there's a quieter factor — declining services. When the local school, clinic or bus route closes, everyday life becomes harder, and the community itself starts to shrink, which pushes more people out in a downward spiral. I'd say it's rarely one reason but a combination that makes staying feel like a choice with no future.",
  "p3-city-rural-02": "I think it's a mixture of both, and the truth depends on who you are. For some people, rural life genuinely is better — cleaner air, lower costs, space, nature and a pace of life that suits them, and there's good evidence that countryside living is linked to lower stress. But the romantic image often ignores the realities: limited jobs, long drives for basic services, and social pressure in small communities where everyone knows your business. The key is that the city and the countryside each have real advantages and real costs, and which one is 'better' is mostly about personality and life stage — not about some absolute truth. The romanticised version usually sells the countryside to people who've never actually lived there.",
  "p3-city-rural-03": "On the whole, yes, I think it is, especially in economic terms. Cities concentrate high-paying jobs, universities and infrastructure, and they benefit from agglomeration — the more talent and capital they attract, the more attractive they become, while rural areas lose their young people and their services in a self-reinforcing decline. Technology has partly helped — remote work and broadband have allowed some people to live rurally while earning urban salaries — but that only helps a minority and can even push up rural housing costs. I'd say the gap is also visible in health and education outcomes. The key is that this isn't inevitable; deliberate investment in rural infrastructure and remote employment could genuinely narrow it.",
  "p3-city-rural-04": "I think the evidence points in both directions. Cities can be mentally stimulating — constant opportunity, culture and social options — and for some people that energy is exactly what they need. But there are real costs: noise, crowding, long commutes and the pressure of visible competition are all linked to higher stress and anxiety, and studies have found higher rates of some mental health conditions in urban populations. The countryside, by contrast, offers quiet, nature and space, which measurably reduce stress hormones, though it can also mean isolation and fewer mental health services. I'd say the key is that it's not simply city versus country — it's about whether your environment matches your personality and whether you have green space and social connection either way.",
  "p3-city-rural-05": "I think rural childhoods offer real advantages: space to play freely, closer contact with nature, stronger community ties and often a safer, slower pace of life. Many adults look back on countryside childhoods with genuine fondness for the freedom. However, the disadvantages are equally real — rural children may have fewer schools to choose from, longer travel for activities, and less exposure to cultural diversity, museums and the kind of opportunities that spark ambition. The key is that both environments shape children differently rather than one being simply better: the city child may gain confidence and worldliness, while the rural child gains resilience and independence. Ideally, children get elements of both, which is why families often move back and forth.",
  "p3-city-rural-06": "Yes, I'd say they should, and there are strong reasons beyond fairness. When rural populations decline, the whole country loses — land is less managed, services collapse and cities absorb the pressure. Concretely, governments can invest in the basics that make rural life viable: reliable broadband, decent transport links, schools, clinics and affordable housing, plus incentives for businesses to locate outside the big cities, like tax breaks or grants. There's also a case for promoting rural industries — agriculture, tourism, renewable energy — which have natural advantages there. I think the key is that making rural areas attractive isn't charity; it's a sensible national strategy, because balanced regional development benefits everyone, including the cities.",
  "p3-globalisation-01": "I'd say on balance the effects have been more positive, though very unevenly distributed. For ordinary consumers, globalisation is a clear win — goods are cheaper, more varied and available year-round, and the internet has opened up information, education and work opportunities that didn't exist before. Hundreds of millions of people in developing countries have been lifted out of poverty through global trade and manufacturing jobs. However, I should acknowledge the negative side honestly: jobs in some industries have moved abroad, local cultures face pressure, and the benefits have flowed disproportionately to the wealthy. So I'd say the key is that globalisation itself is neither good nor bad — its effects depend entirely on how governments manage it.",
  "p3-globalisation-02": "I think it does pose a genuine threat, though the mechanism is more indirect than people often imagine. Globalisation doesn't outlaw minority languages; it simply makes the dominant language overwhelmingly more useful — in business, education and entertainment — so young people rationally choose to spend their time in English or another major language, and languages die when children stop speaking them. UNESCO estimates that a large share of the world's languages are endangered. However, I'd say globalisation also offers tools for preservation: minority communities now use social media and apps to teach and promote their languages to a global audience, something impossible before. So the threat is real, but so is the counter-movement — survival largely depends on whether communities invest in transmission.",
  "p3-globalisation-03": "I think the effects are almost a mirror image. In developed countries, globalisation has tended to shift manufacturing and routine service jobs abroad where labour is cheaper, which has cost many workers their jobs and pushed wages down in those sectors — that's a major source of political resentment. In developing countries, the same process has created millions of factory and service jobs, lifting incomes and reducing poverty, though often with poor working conditions. The key is that the winners and losers are not countries but workers within countries: a software engineer in either place benefits from global markets, while a factory worker in the developed world faces competition from a counterpart earning far less. Managing that transition fairly is the central challenge.",
  "p3-globalisation-04": "I think the opposition is very understandable when you look beyond the aggregate statistics. The economic benefits of globalisation are real but diffuse — everyone pays slightly less for goods — while the costs are concentrated and painful: a town loses its factory, a worker loses his career, and the compensation rarely arrives. There's also a cultural and psychological dimension: globalisation feels like distant forces deciding the fate of your community, and people resent losing control over their own lives, even when the outcomes are statistically positive. And inequality matters — when the gains flow to the top, the majority can rationally conclude that globalisation is rigged. So I'd say opposition isn't irrational; it's a reasonable response when benefits are shared thinly but costs are borne personally.",
  "p3-globalisation-05": "It depends, I think, on the industry and the stage of development. Protecting infant industries — new sectors that need time to become competitive — is a well-established strategy that worked for many countries, including Japan and South Korea. However, permanent protection usually backfires: sheltered industries become complacent, costs rise for consumers, and the country falls behind in the long run. The key is that protection should be temporary and strategic, not defensive. There's also a nuance — every country protects something, whether it's agriculture, national security industries or cultural sectors, so the real question isn't whether to protect but what, how long and at what cost to consumers.",
  "p3-globalisation-06": "I think the economic challenges are the more tangible ones: jobs and wages are now set in a global market, so workers compete with counterparts around the world, and a crisis anywhere — a supply chain breakdown, a financial shock — quickly becomes a crisis everywhere. The cultural challenges are subtler but just as real: identities can feel diluted as local customs give way to a global consumer culture, and communities worry about losing what makes them distinct. There's also the challenge of balancing openness with security and sovereignty. I'd say the overarching difficulty is that globalisation demands constant adaptation — individuals, companies and governments all have to keep learning and adjusting, and the pace of change itself is a source of stress.",
  "p3-elderly-01": "I think support should combine practical services with genuine social inclusion. Practically, that means accessible healthcare, affordable housing, transport that works for people with limited mobility, and financial security through pensions that actually keep pace with living costs. But the meaningful part matters just as much: loneliness is a serious problem among older people, so community centres, volunteer visiting schemes and intergenerational programmes make a real difference. I'd also say that older people want to contribute, not just be cared for — many would happily mentor, volunteer or work part-time if opportunities existed. The key is that support should treat the elderly as active members of society, not passive recipients of care.",
  "p3-elderly-02": "Absolutely, and I'd argue their role is often underestimated. They carry experience and institutional memory that no textbook or database can fully replace — in workplaces, they've seen cycles of boom and bust and know what actually works. Socially, they're the anchors of families and communities, providing childcare, wisdom and continuity, and research shows that societies with strong intergenerational ties are healthier for everyone. Their role as carers is enormous too: many grandparents effectively keep young families afloat. I'd say the challenge is that modern society has become age-segregated, so the value older people offer is less visible than it could be. The role is there; we've just stopped making space for it.",
  "p3-elderly-03": "I think it's a paradox of density — cities are full of people, but they're full of strangers. Older people often lose their social networks through retirement and the deaths of friends, and the urban lifestyle, where people move frequently and interact mainly with colleagues, makes it hard to rebuild them. Mobility is another factor: if it's difficult to leave the house — because of stairs, poor transport or safety fears — being surrounded by millions of people doesn't help at all. There's also a generational mismatch: much city life is designed around the young, from late-night venues to digital services that older people may not use. So the isolation isn't about being alone; it's about being disconnected in the middle of a crowd.",
  "p3-elderly-04": "Yes, I think they often are, especially in fast-moving industries. There's a cultural bias toward youth and novelty — new ideas, new technology — and older workers can be stereotyped as slow to adapt, even though the evidence shows experienced workers are often more reliable, better at judgement and stronger at handling complexity. Part of the problem is how we measure value: workplaces reward speed and digital fluency, which favour the young, while the hard-won wisdom of older colleagues — knowing what not to do, reading people, navigating crises — is harder to quantify. There's also a structural issue: when older workers are pushed out, their knowledge walks out the door with them. I'd say that's a genuine loss for organisations.",
  "p3-elderly-05": "I think the benefits run in both directions, which is what makes it so valuable. Young people gain something that's hard to find elsewhere: lived experience, perspective, patience and practical wisdom — research even suggests children who interact regularly with grandparents develop better social and emotional skills. Older people gain the opposite benefit: energy, new perspectives, and a sense of being needed and connected, which directly combats loneliness and depression. There's also a community-level effect — such interaction breaks down stereotypes on both sides, so age becomes less of a dividing line. And in practical terms, it's often a two-way exchange of help, with older people providing childcare and younger people providing digital support. Everyone wins, really.",
  "p3-elderly-06": "Yes, I'd say strongly, and there are several reasons it's worth the investment. Activity — social, physical and mental — is one of the best protections against the decline that people fear about old age, so programmes that keep older people engaged can actually reduce healthcare costs in the long run. Concretely, governments can fund community centres, subsidise classes and sports for older adults, support volunteering schemes, and remove barriers like age limits on certain activities. There's also a fairness argument: older people have paid taxes and built the community for decades, so they've earned the opportunity to stay part of it. The key is that these programmes should be designed with older people, not just for them.",
  "p3-child-education-01": "I'd say it depends on what 'formal schooling' means. If it means structured, desk-based instruction with tests, then I think earlier is generally worse — young children learn through play, movement and relationships, and pushing academic content too early can create stress without real advantage. Research from countries that start formal schooling late, like Finland, shows children catch up quickly and often develop better motivation. However, if 'schooling' means high-quality early childhood education — socialisation, language-rich environments and guided play — then earlier can be genuinely beneficial, especially for disadvantaged children. So the key is the quality and style of the experience rather than the starting age itself.",
  "p3-child-education-02": "I think the most effective approach is learning through experience — letting children touch, build, experiment and play rather than just listen. Young children don't learn abstract ideas well from lectures; they learn by doing, and by asking questions that adults take seriously. There's strong evidence for play-based learning, where children explore guided environments and pick up literacy and numeracy almost incidentally through games and stories. The social dimension matters too: children learn enormously from each other, so mixed-ability groups and collaborative tasks help. I'd also say the relationship with the teacher is foundational — children learn best from adults they trust and feel safe with, so warmth and responsiveness are not optional extras.",
  "p3-child-education-03": "For young children, I'd say yes, clearly — play is how their brains are wired to learn. Through play, children practise problem-solving, language, negotiation and emotional regulation, and they do it with intrinsic motivation, so the learning sticks much better than forced instruction. Research on the developing brain supports this: exploration and social play build the neural connections that formal learning later depends on. However, I should be careful — I'm not saying formal instruction is useless; it's just that it becomes effective at an older age, when children have the attention and abstract reasoning to benefit. The key is sequencing: rich, guided play first, structured instruction later, and the evidence suggests trying to skip the first stage backfires.",
  "p3-child-education-04": "I think it's because the early years are when the brain develops fastest — a huge proportion of neural connections form before age five, and what happens in those years literally shapes the architecture of the brain. Early education isn't mainly about teaching facts; it's about building foundations: language, self-regulation, the ability to focus, and social skills like sharing and empathy, which predict later success in school and life remarkably well. There's also a powerful equity argument — high-quality early education narrows the gap between advantaged and disadvantaged children, with benefits that last into adulthood. I'd say the key insight is that the return on investment in early childhood is higher than at almost any other stage of education.",
  "p3-child-education-05": "I think the starting point is modelling — children catch attitudes from the adults around them, so parents who read for pleasure and ask curious questions raise children who do the same. Choice matters enormously: children who get to explore what interests them develop intrinsic motivation, whereas forced tasks teach them to see learning as a chore to be endured. Praise also needs to be handled carefully — praising effort and process rather than intelligence encourages children to embrace challenges instead of fearing failure. And the environment matters: plenty of books, materials and open-ended questions, plus time for unstructured exploration. I'd say the key is making learning feel like discovery rather than assessment.",
  "p3-child-education-06": "I think the effects are often the opposite of what parents intend. Children under intense academic pressure frequently develop anxiety, sleep problems and stress-related illness, and the pressure can kill intrinsic motivation — many such children burn out or lose interest in learning entirely by adolescence. There's also a narrower development: when time is filled with tutoring and test prep, children miss out on play, sport and socialising, which are essential for emotional and social skills that matter just as much in adult life. And the pressure signals something damaging — that the child's worth depends on performance. I'd say the cruel irony is that moderate pressure can help, but excess pressure produces fragile, anxious adults, not high achievers.",
  "p3-consumerism-01": "On the whole, I'd say yes — or at least, we live in a system that pushes materialism harder than ever. Advertising, social media and even casual conversation constantly link happiness to possessions, and people's status is increasingly read from what they own and display. At the same time, I think it's worth being fair: research suggests people actually value experiences and relationships more than stuff, but the culture keeps nudging them toward consumption anyway. There's also a generational shift — younger people seem more interested in experiences and sustainability than their parents were, which suggests materialism is not a fixed human trait but a response to the environment. Change the environment, and the values shift.",
  "p3-consumerism-02": "I think it's the gap between rational knowledge and emotional impulse. People know the theory — save, budget, live within your means — but spending decisions are made in the moment, and that's exactly when the environment is engineered against us: limited-time offers, buy-now-pay-later, targeted ads and one-click payment all remove the friction that used to make us pause. There's also an emotional dimension — spending is often driven by stress, boredom, or the desire to feel in control or to keep up with peers, and these feelings override the rational brain. And card payments make spending feel abstract; the pain of handing over cash has been engineered away. So overspending isn't a character flaw so much as a predictable response to a system designed to exploit it.",
  "p3-consumerism-03": "I think it's both, but the balance varies by product and person. Some purchases genuinely improve life — a good mattress, a bike, tools, education — and I'd say most people believe they're buying for themselves. But a huge share of consumption is social signalling: the car, the watch, the handbag, the latest phone are partly about status and belonging, and social media has intensified this by making everyone's purchases visible to everyone. The subtle part is that people rarely admit it, even to themselves — we dress up status purchases as rational ones. The key is that the same product can serve both purposes; what matters is the motivation behind it, which is usually a mix we don't fully acknowledge.",
  "p3-consumerism-04": "I think it's a combination of economic structure and cultural feedback. Economically, modern economies depend on consumption for growth — companies need ever-increasing sales, so entire industries are devoted to creating demand, and governments encourage spending as a measure of health. Culturally, consumption has filled a gap: as religion, community and tradition have weakened, buying has become a way people define themselves and find meaning — the idea that identity is something you build through what you own. Social media then amplifies the loop, making everyone's consumption visible and creating constant comparison. I'd say the key is that consumer culture isn't a natural preference but a cultivated one, and it's so dominant because it's so profitable.",
  "p3-consumerism-05": "I think they shape them in three ways. First, they normalise constant spending — when everyone in your feed is showing purchases and 'treating themselves', spending stops feeling like a choice and starts feeling like the default way to live. Second, they change what possessions mean: advertising links products to identity and emotion, so a watch isn't a timepiece but a statement about who you are, and buying becomes a way to feel the way you want to feel. Third, they distort our sense of what's normal — influencers display wealth and consumption that distort perceptions of average life. The result is that money becomes less about security and more about performance, and satisfaction always seems to be one purchase away.",
  "p3-consumerism-06": "The consequences are severe and compounding. Every product requires raw materials, energy and transport, so constant consumption means constant resource extraction — mining, deforestation, water use — and mountains of waste, much of it plastic that persists for centuries. There's also the emissions problem: the production and shipping of goods is a major share of global carbon emissions, so our shopping habits are literally heating the planet. And the culture of replacement makes it worse — products designed to be discarded mean the whole cycle restarts faster. I'd say the key is that consumer culture externalises these costs: the environmental price is paid by everyone, but the profit and convenience go to the consumer and the company, which is precisely why the system is so hard to change.",
  "p3-language-learning-01": "I think the motivations fall into practical and personal categories. Practically, languages open career doors — in a globalised job market, bilingual employees are genuinely more valuable, and for work, study or migration, a language is often a requirement rather than an option. Personally, many people learn for travel, for relationships, or simply for the pleasure of it — and there's growing interest in learning languages for cultural connection, like understanding films or music in the original. I'd also say that in an era of instant machine translation, some people wonder why bother — but the answer is that translation gives you the words, not the relationships. Speaking someone's language changes how they treat you in a way an app never will.",
  "p3-language-learning-02": "I'd say yes in terms of ultimate outcome, but it's not the simple advantage people imagine. Children do have a genuine edge: their brains are more plastic, they absorb pronunciation and grammar without fear of making mistakes, and they typically learn through immersion, which is the most effective method. Adults, however, have their own advantages — motivation, self-discipline, existing language knowledge and sophisticated learning strategies — so adults often progress faster in the early stages. The key is that children usually end up more native-like, especially in accent, but adults absolutely can reach high fluency. The real difference is opportunity: children have time and immersion; adults have to engineer both, which is why it feels harder even if the brain isn't the main obstacle.",
  "p3-language-learning-03": "I think it will remain dominant for the foreseeable future, because language dominance is driven by network effects — the more people speak it, the more valuable it is to learn, and that loop is hard to break. English benefits from being the language of science, business, technology and the internet, and that position is self-reinforcing. However, I'd say it's unlikely to stay the only global language. China's economic rise has made Mandarin increasingly attractive, Spanish and Arabic already have enormous speaker bases, and machine translation may reduce the pressure on people to learn English at all. So the likely future is less English monopoly and more multilingualism — English as the default second language, but not the only route to the world.",
  "p3-language-learning-04": "I think it changes your worldview in a surprisingly profound way. Most obviously, languages carry different ways of organising reality — some languages don't distinguish colours the same way, or express time and relationships differently — so learning one reveals that the way you think is not the only way, which is humbling and liberating at once. There's also a cultural layer: a language is saturated with its speakers' history, humour and values, so you start understanding jokes, references and social norms that were invisible before. And practically, learning another language makes you more aware of your own — you notice its oddities and limitations. I'd say the deepest change is empathy: you literally learn to inhabit another way of seeing, and that stays with you.",
  "p3-language-learning-05": "I'd say yes, at least through secondary school, and the arguments are strong. Language learning has documented cognitive benefits — better memory, attention and even delayed cognitive decline — and in a globalised world, some exposure to another language and culture is part of a complete education. There's also a fairness argument: if languages are optional, they become the preserve of privileged students whose families can afford extra tuition, so compulsory provision equalises opportunity. However, I should add a caveat — compulsory shouldn't mean one-size-fits-all. Schools should offer choices and emphasise communication over grammar drills, because a compulsory subject that students hate defeats the purpose. The goal is opening the door, not forcing everyone through it.",
  "p3-language-learning-06": "I think the biggest difficulties are time and confidence rather than brain power. Adults have jobs, families and responsibilities, so they can't give a language the hours of daily exposure that children naturally get, and progress becomes painfully slow by comparison. Confidence is the second barrier: adults hate making mistakes and feel embarrassed speaking imperfectly, which stops them practising — and practice is the whole game. There's also the fossilisation problem: adults' pronunciation and grammar patterns harden, so reaching native-like fluency is genuinely harder. And finally, adults' lives are conducted in their first language — without the immersion that forces the brain to adapt, it's easy to default back. The key is that none of these are insurmountable; they just require a strategy.",
  "p3-news-media-01": "I think it's important because the online world has removed the filters we used to rely on. In the past, information passed through editors, fact-checkers and publishers who had reputations to protect; now anyone can publish anything instantly, including content designed to deceive. The stakes are real — misinformation has influenced elections, spread dangerous health advice and fuelled violence, and once a false story is shared, the correction never catches up. There's also the algorithm problem: platforms show us content based on engagement, not accuracy, so the most outrageous claims are often the most visible. I'd say the key is that questioning what we see isn't cynicism; it's basic self-defence in an environment where the old gatekeepers are gone.",
  "p3-news-media-02": "The change is dramatic — news now travels at the speed of a share rather than a news cycle. In the past, a story appeared on television at six or in the morning paper; now a major event can be reported by a witness on the ground within minutes, and reach millions through shares and algorithms before any professional outlet has even confirmed it. This speed has real consequences: it's a genuine asset in emergencies, when early warnings can save lives, but it's also a serious problem, because unverified rumours spread as fast as confirmed facts. By the time the truth emerges, the false version has already shaped public opinion. I'd say the key is that speed has outrun verification, and we haven't yet built mechanisms to keep up.",
  "p3-news-media-03": "On the whole, no — though it's a valuable complement. Citizen journalism's great strength is being there: a bystander with a phone can capture events that professionals haven't reached, and in repressive countries, ordinary people often provide the only record of what's happening. But reliability is a different matter — citizen journalists usually lack training in verification, context and balance, and they may have personal agendas, so their reports can be partial or wrong. Professional journalism, at its best, has institutional discipline: editors, fact-checking, ethical standards and accountability. I'd say the ideal relationship is complementary — citizen footage and eyewitness accounts feeding into professional newsrooms that verify and contextualise them. Alone, citizen journalism is raw material; it becomes journalism through professional processing.",
  "p3-news-media-04": "I think the dangers are both personal and social. Personally, a confirmation-bias diet gives you a distorted picture of reality — you come to believe the whole world agrees with you, which makes you overconfident and poorly prepared for how things actually are. Socially, it feeds polarisation: when different groups live in completely different information worlds, they stop sharing common facts, and genuine debate becomes impossible because people are arguing from different realities. There's also a subtle radicalisation risk — algorithms that keep feeding you increasingly extreme versions of your views can pull people toward positions they'd never have reached on their own. I'd say the key is that a healthy democracy depends on citizens encountering views they disagree with, and echo chambers are corrosive precisely because they prevent that.",
  "p3-news-media-05": "I'd say yes, but the responsibility needs to be carefully designed, because the alternatives are both bad. Platforms clearly profit from engagement, and misinformation drives engagement, so leaving them completely unaccountable means giving them a financial incentive to spread falsehoods — that's clearly wrong. On the other hand, holding them fully liable as publishers would push them to over-censor, chilling legitimate speech and debate. So the key is a middle path: platforms should be accountable for their amplification systems — the algorithms that decide what millions of people see — through transparency, independent audits and effective takedown of clear harms, like health misinformation and election lies, without making them the arbiters of all truth. Responsibility, yes; total control, no.",
  "p3-news-media-06": "I think it starts with a habit of asking basic questions before accepting anything: Who published this, and what's their track record? Is there a primary source, or is it just a claim repeated by other people? Does the headline match the actual content? There's also the cross-checking habit — seeing how different outlets, especially those with different leanings, cover the same story is one of the most powerful tools there is. And I'd say critical thinking is partly emotional: learning to pause when something makes you angry or thrilled, because that's exactly when your judgement is weakest. Media literacy education in schools would help enormously, but the essential skill — comfortable scepticism, not cynicism — is something people can practise daily."
};
